<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Waltrude</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 688</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Saint Waudru</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint Waltrude was a Frankish noblewoman who founded a religious community at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> in Hainaut in the seventh century, around which the town grew. Married to Vincent Madelgarius and the mother of children also venerated as saints, she withdrew to a life of prayer. She is the patron saint of Mons, and her relics are carried through the town each year in the Car d'Or procession.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-waltrude.jpg" alt="Saint Waltrude" width="320" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jmh2o · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soignies_-_buste_de_sainte_Waudru_-_bois_polychrom%C3%A9_et_dor%C3%A9_-_1691-1710_-_2025-09-28_-_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Waltrude, known in French as Waudru, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> as Waldetrudis and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as Valdetrudis, was born at Cousolre in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> into a wealthy and influential noble family. Her parents, Walbert IV and Bertille, were later venerated as saints. According to Alban Butler she was the sister of Aldegonde, foundress of Maubeuge Abbey, and she was a cousin of Saint Aye.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>She married Vincent Madelgarius, Count of Hainault, and they had four children: Aldetrude, abbess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maubeuge">Maubeuge</a>; Landry of Soignies, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>; Madelberte, who succeeded her sister as abbess of Maubeuge; and Dentelin. Around 642 Madelgarius founded the monastery of Hautmont, and Waltrude persuaded Saint Ghislain to set up an oratory at a place called Ursidongus, now Saint-Ghislain.</p><p>Around 656, after the death of the young Dentelin, the couple agreed to separate and enter religious life. Madelgarius went to Hautmont and became a Benedictine monk. Hydulphe of Hainault, the husband of her cousin Aye, built her a cell and chapel near Ghislain's oratory, where her sister Aldegonde sometimes visited her. Her biography praises her for a vow to free captives: she paid their ransom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> from her own purse and sent them home to their families.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tradition places her death on 9 April 688, and she was proclaimed holy by popular acclaim. Her husband and all four of their children, as well as her sister Aldegund, were also declared saints. She is the patron saint of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> and of Herentals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, and each city has a large medieval church bearing her name. Her shrine is kept in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/collegiate-church-of-saint-waltrude">Collegiate Church of Saint Waltrude</a> at Mons and drawn through the streets by horses on the gilded car d'or during the Ducasse de Mons festival.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Waltrude” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hainaut's towns and villages lived under written charters, and the county developed a strong regional identity expressed in its Estates, its customary law and its devotion to local saints such as <b>Waltrude</b>, the patron of Mons, whose relics are still carried through the town each year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cousolre, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Mons, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.25,4.15&amp;year=678" aria-label="Cousolre on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Saint Waltrude"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5227 5823h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5227 5823h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.25,4.15&amp;year=678">What was here in 678 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
